## Finding and fix path

The current-source five-minute capture reproduces the AC-4 maximum-gap failure: 6,050 selected matching frames over 302.506666 seconds, 20.000523 fps mean cadence, 89.163715 ms maximum EOF gap, and 753 of 6,049 gaps above 60 ms. Direct V4L2 timestamps were regular in a separate capture, while a no-IPC FFmpeg raw-pipe reader reproduced gaps above 60 ms. The evidence localizes the measured jitter, at medium confidence, to the FFmpeg raw-output and full-frame pipe-read completion path before IPC. It does not separate FFmpeg decode/output timing from host/Python scheduling.

The bounded BUILD fix path is to carry and validate each frame's monotonic source timestamp with its decoded NV12 payload, publish the verified source EOF timestamp, and retain actual read/publication latency separately. Do not smooth timestamps or synthesize a fixed 20 fps schedule. First verify frame-to-timestamp alignment, then rerun the five-minute AC-4 acceptance probe. If source timestamp mapping is unavailable or cadence still fails, investigate FFmpeg and host scheduling rather than treating timestamp relabeling as a fix.

## Open evidence limits

- AC-4 cadence still fails on current source. AC-6 physical reconnect/sibling continuity and AC-8 four-camera soak/CPU headroom remain unverified because only one decoder is available.
- FFmpeg decode/output timing and host/Python scheduling remain unresolved. The investigation package names the same-frame correlated observation that would distinguish them.
- Product source remains unchanged. The active read-only guard must be formally released by Admiral before BUILD edits any source files.

